    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Area and line length preservation, constant fault offset, and constant bed thickness as used to constrain kinematically-based models for structural restorations of balanced cross sections are not Laws of Nature. In contrast, mechanically based models are grounded in conservation of mass, momentum and energy, which are such Laws. Examples of mechanically-based restoration are given using the boundary element method and the finite element method. We conclude that mechanically-based models used for structural restorations can provide important constraints on the geometric interpretation of folds and faults as imaged in seismic reflection data or portrayed on geological maps and cross sections.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
